# Native differential test of string-replace.js. The modules come from
# ctcompile_add_native_pipeline in Native.cmake, which lowers the program to
# native C++ (writing no module while any function carries `ctnative.not_native`)
# plus a variant printed with deduced `auto` types and one with a wrong type pin.
# compilation-unit.py compiles a module standalone, checks with nm that it calls
# no interpreter, runs it, and compares every global with the interpreter's.
# compile-clean.py checks a warning-free build on both toolchains with a
# provenance comment over every definition. print-deduced.py checks that the
# deduced module differs only in type spellings and that the wrong pin fails.
# A `not ... --mutate G` line adds one to global G in the C++ and expects the
# comparison to fail naming G, proving the comparison can catch a difference.

# String.prototype.replace with a RegExp literal and a function. The callback
# bound to its captures is an `auto` local (`std::bind_front`), whose type has
# no spelling for the printing policy to pin, so print-deduced.py is not run,
# as for array-callbacks.test; the deduced module still builds and runs.
# RUN: %compilation_unit --module %{obj}/string_replace.pipeline.emitc.mlir --js %S/string-replace.js --work %t --name string_replace --core-build %build
# RUN: not %compilation_unit --module %{obj}/string_replace.pipeline.emitc.mlir --js %S/string-replace.js --work %t --name string_replace_off_by_one --core-build %build --mutate dashedLength11 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=OFF-BY-ONE
# OFF-BY-ONE: global 'dashedLength11' differs
# RUN: %compilation_unit --module %{obj}/string_replace.pipeline.deduced.emitc.mlir --js %S/string-replace.js --work %t --name string_replace_deduced --core-build %build
# RUN: %compile_clean --module %{obj}/string_replace.pipeline.emitc.mlir --work %t --name string_replace
# RUN: %compile_clean --module %{obj}/string_replace.pipeline.deduced.emitc.mlir --work %t --name string_replace_deduced
# The spelling: the JavaScript names, the literal pattern and the bound callback.
# RUN: ctjs-translate --mlir-to-cpp %{obj}/string_replace.pipeline.emitc.mlir | FileCheck %s
# CHECK-DAG: #include "ctcompile/CTNative/Runtime/RegExp.hpp"
# CHECK-DAG: ctnative::String.prototype.replace.call(
# CHECK-DAG: ctnative::RegExp("[A-Z]", "g")
# CHECK-DAG: ctnative::RegExp("o", "")
# CHECK-DAG: std::bind_front(
# CHECK-DAG: ctnative::String.prototype.toLowerCase.call(
